WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.535
This chapter covers carbon monoxide.

2
00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:12.386
We start with the carboxyhemoglobin levels, normal, smokers and poisoning, and why carbon monoxide harms.

3
00:00:12.386 --> 00:00:21.491
Then how to measure it, and why a normal PaO2 and a normal pulse oximeter reading do not rule out poisoning.

4
00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:35.400
We finish with treatment: one hundred percent oxygen right away, the half-lives on room air, oxygen and hyperbaric oxygen, and when to consider a hyperbaric chamber.

5
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:56.342
RCP 203 Lessons 3a and 3b give one scale: one to three percent is within normal limits, five to ten percent is typical of smokers, ten to fifteen percent of heavy smokers, and above twenty percent is carbon monoxide poisoning.

6
00:00:56.342 --> 00:01:11.301
Lesson 1 gives another: below one and a half percent is a normal level, and three to six percent is normal for smokers, inner city dwellers and people near industrial areas.

7
00:01:11.301 --> 00:01:24.011
After smoke inhalation, a level of three to four percent or more in a nonsmoker, or ten percent or more in a smoker, is outside the expected range.

8
00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:29.459
The two versions differ on normal and on smokers, so know both.

9
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:33.350
Above twenty percent means poisoning in both.

10
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:50.631
Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin about two hundred to two hundred fifty times more strongly than oxygen, per the burn deck, and RCP 203 Lesson 1 says about two hundred ten times.

11
00:01:50.631 --> 00:01:59.276
It forms carboxyhemoglobin, which leaves less hemoglobin to carry oxygen, causing tissue hypoxia.

12
00:01:59.276 --> 00:02:09.614
It also shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, so the oxygen that is carried is held more tightly.

13
00:02:09.614 --> 00:02:15.496
Hess adds that at fifty percent, unconsciousness routinely occurs.

14
00:02:15.496 --> 00:02:26.280
Whitaker describes headache, nausea and vomiting at twenty to thirty percent, and loss of sensorium and coma above forty.

15
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.667
No source prints a full symptom table.

16
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:38.166
Measure carboxyhemoglobin with CO-oximetry, a blood gas run on a CO-oximeter.

17
00:02:38.166 --> 00:02:46.095
Smoke exposure with a normal PaO2 and a normal saturation does not rule out poisoning.

18
00:02:46.095 --> 00:02:52.641
The PaO2 can be normal, because it measures oxygen dissolved in plasma.

19
00:02:52.641 --> 00:03:10.067
A conventional pulse oximeter reads misleadingly normal or high, because it cannot reliably tell oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so the pulse oximeter reads above the true saturation.

20
00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:15.414
CO-oximetry measures each hemoglobin component separately.

21
00:03:15.414 --> 00:03:24.542
Hess adds that the oximeter overestimates the true saturation by about the carboxyhemoglobin level.

22
00:03:25.967 --> 00:03:34.765
RCP 103 gives a bedside correction: subtract the carboxyhemoglobin from the pulse oximeter reading.

23
00:03:34.765 --> 00:03:44.096
A saturation of ninety percent with seven percent carboxyhemoglobin is a true saturation of eighty three.

24
00:03:44.096 --> 00:04:00.892
RCP 203 Lesson 3b adds an exhaled carbon monoxide estimate: parts per million divided by six is about the carboxyhemoglobin percent, so forty eight parts per million is about eight percent.

25
00:04:00.892 --> 00:04:06.758
That is for moderate poisoning in conscious, cooperative patients.

26
00:04:06.758 --> 00:04:22.665
Hess adds that newer multi-wavelength oximeters report a carbon monoxide reading, but its accuracy is plus or minus six percent, so it should not be used for triage or management.

27
00:04:22.665 --> 00:04:29.242
A low reading never rules out poisoning; confirm with a blood measurement.

28
00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:32.677
Pause and work it out.

29
00:04:32.677 --> 00:04:40.259
A fire victim has a pulse oximeter reading of ninety six percent and a normal PaO2.

30
00:04:40.259 --> 00:04:45.558
A CO-oximeter shows twenty five percent carboxyhemoglobin.

31
00:04:45.558 --> 00:04:52.867
Is carbon monoxide poisoning ruled out, and roughly what is the true saturation?

32
00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:59.767
[6-second practice pause]

33
00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:03.119
No, poisoning is not ruled out.

34
00:05:03.119 --> 00:05:09.929
Twenty five percent is above twenty, so this is carbon monoxide poisoning.

35
00:05:09.929 --> 00:05:20.971
The normal PaO2 reflects only dissolved oxygen, and the pulse oximeter cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin.

36
00:05:20.971 --> 00:05:31.001
By the bedside correction, ninety six minus twenty five gives a true saturation of about seventy one percent.

37
00:05:31.001 --> 00:05:35.142
Start one hundred percent oxygen immediately.

38
00:05:36.567 --> 00:05:45.905
Give one hundred percent oxygen immediately; it speeds the displacement of carbon monoxide from hemoglobin.

39
00:05:45.905 --> 00:05:54.895
Chapter eleven says carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning get the highest possible FiO2 in emergencies.

40
00:05:54.895 --> 00:06:11.564
A spontaneously breathing patient gets a nonrebreathing mask with a tight seal and the reservoir kept inflated, and Hess gives fifteen liters per minute regardless of the saturation and PaO2.

41
00:06:11.564 --> 00:06:19.855
A patient who cannot protect the airway or ventilate is intubated on an FiO2 of one point zero.

42
00:06:19.855 --> 00:06:30.067
In mild to moderate poisoning, continue one hundred percent oxygen until the carboxyhemoglobin is below five percent.

43
00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:38.567
The burn deck gives the carboxyhemoglobin half-life by what the patient breathes.

44
00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:42.425
On room air, it is about four to five hours.

45
00:06:42.425 --> 00:06:47.772
On one hundred percent oxygen, about sixty to ninety minutes.

46
00:06:47.772 --> 00:06:52.594
With hyperbaric oxygen, about twenty to thirty minutes.

47
00:06:52.594 --> 00:07:05.568
Hess gives about five hours on room air, about seventy four minutes on oxygen, with a wide range, and less than thirty minutes at three atmospheres.

48
00:07:05.568 --> 00:07:11.617
Use the deck's sixty to ninety minutes on one hundred percent oxygen.

49
00:07:13.033 --> 00:07:24.305
Consider hyperbaric oxygen for severe carbon monoxide poisoning, especially with significant neurologic depression or cardiac involvement.

50
00:07:24.305 --> 00:07:29.451
The decision is not based on the carboxyhemoglobin level alone.

51
00:07:29.451 --> 00:07:39.008
A typical protocol is three atmospheres first, then two or three, for ninety minutes, with two ten minute air breaks.

52
00:07:39.008 --> 00:07:53.302
Do not delay oxygen while deciding: give one hundred percent oxygen, measure carboxyhemoglobin by CO-oximetry, assess the severity, and then transfer for hyperbaric treatment.

53
00:07:53.302 --> 00:08:08.494
Hess indicates hyperbaric oxygen for carbon monoxide poisoning, but notes that its advantage over normal-pressure one hundred percent oxygen is debated and its use remains controversial.

54
00:08:08.494 --> 00:08:12.333
Use the deck: consider it for severe poisoning.

55
00:08:13.733 --> 00:08:14.496
One more.

56
00:08:14.496 --> 00:08:22.380
A patient with carbon monoxide poisoning is confused and has new chest pain with ECG changes.

57
00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:32.383
Should you wait for the carboxyhemoglobin level before starting treatment, and is hyperbaric oxygen worth considering?

58
00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:39.300
[6-second practice pause]

59
00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:40.830
Do not wait.

60
00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:49.074
Start one hundred percent oxygen immediately, then measure the carboxyhemoglobin by CO-oximetry.

61
00:08:49.074 --> 00:09:07.450
Confusion is neurologic depression, and chest pain with ECG changes suggests cardiac involvement, so this is severe poisoning, and hyperbaric oxygen should be considered, whatever the exact carboxyhemoglobin level.

62
00:09:08.867 --> 00:09:11.322
Here is the chapter in brief.

63
00:09:11.322 --> 00:09:20.974
Normal carboxyhemoglobin is about one to three percent, smokers run higher, and above twenty percent is poisoning.

64
00:09:20.974 --> 00:09:30.203
Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin about two hundred times more strongly than oxygen and shifts the curve left.

65
00:09:30.203 --> 00:09:44.766
A normal PaO2 and pulse oximeter reading do not rule it out; measure with CO-oximetry, and subtract the carboxyhemoglobin from the oximeter reading for the true saturation.

66
00:09:44.766 --> 00:09:55.688
Treat with one hundred percent oxygen immediately, by a tight nonrebreather or intubation, until the level is below five percent.

67
00:09:55.688 --> 00:10:10.167
The half-life drops from hours on room air to sixty to ninety minutes on oxygen, and hyperbaric oxygen is considered for severe poisoning with neurologic or cardiac signs.
